The founding date is uncertain, with some records suggesting {{ill|1000|ja|1000th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Chōhō 2) or {{ill|1023|ja|1023rd year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Jian 3).{{#tag:ref|''Atsuta Daijingū Shintai Den Monogatarigaki''{{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=179}}|group="Note"}} During the reign of {{ill|Emperor Ichijō|ja|一条天皇|12=draft|12=draft}}, Japan experienced a severe {{ill|epidemic|ja|疫病|12=draft|12=draft}}, and in Kyoto, a {{ill|Goryō-e|ja|御霊会|12=draft|12=draft}} was held in {{ill|1001|ja|1001st year|12=draft|12=draft}}.{{#tag:ref|''"On the 9th day of the 5th month, a ritual was held at {{ill|Shinano|ja|紫野|12=draft|12=draft}} to pacify the epidemic deity. It was called Goryō-e."''
(''Nihon Kiryaku,'' later Ichijō Tennō section){{Sfn|Kokushi Taikei 5|1897|pp=1046-1047}}|group="Note"}} It is believed that Minami Shingū-sha was established in response to this epidemic crisis.{{#tag:ref|''"In the third year of Jian (1023), an epidemic spread nationwide, so it was enshrined for pacification."''
(''Shaden''){{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=245}}|group="Note"}} However, little is known about the shrine's activities during the {{ill|Heian period|ja|平安時代|12=draft|12=draft}} and {{ill|Kamakura period|ja|鎌倉時代|12=draft|12=draft}}.

The shrine was reconstructed in {{ill|1291|ja|1291st year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Shōō 4),{{Sfn|City History: Shrines and Temples|1915|p=161}} and a document from {{ill|1431|ja|1431st year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Eikyō 3) mentions "Minami Shingū."{{Sfn|Fukada|1898|p=18}} Some scholars theorize that Minami Shingū-sha did not exist until the mid-{{ill|Muromachi period|ja|室町時代|12=draft|12=draft}}, and that it was originally the site of {{ill|Magowaka Mikoshi Shrine|ja|孫若御子神社|12=draft|12=draft}}, which was later replaced by Minami Shingū-sha under the patronage of the {{ill|Oda clan|ja|織田氏|12=draft|12=draft}}, who were devout worshippers of Gozu Tennō.{{Sfn|Tsuda|1916|p=71}}

Despite being a subsidiary shrine of {{ill|Atsuta Shrine|ja|熱田神宮|12=draft|12=draft}}, Minami Shingū-sha had a unique role as a deity of epidemics and was a major center for popular {{ill|Gion faith|ja|祇園信仰|12=draft|12=draft}} and {{ill|Tsushima faith|ja|津島信仰|12=draft|12=draft}}. It was also the local {{ill|Ujigami|ja|氏神|12=draft|12=draft}} for the merchants and commoners of Atsuta.{{Sfn|Historic Sites and Cultural Properties|1991|p=70}}

In {{ill|1686|ja|1686th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Jōkyō 3), during a major restoration of Atsuta Shrine under the supervision of {{ill|Sakai Tadaakira|ja|酒井忠挙|12=draft|12=draft}}, doubts were raised about the legitimacy of Minami Shingū-sha. However, the shrine’s existence was defended by Atsuta Shrine officials who described it as an important "divine mystery" (神秘).{{#tag:ref|''"Minami Shingū is a shrine of special divine mystery."''
(''Atsuta Miya Gozoei Jōkyōki''){{Sfn|Zōei Senkū-hen Jō|1980|p=195}}|group="Note"}}

