'''Minami Shingū-sha''' (南新宮社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|ja|神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinto shrine|qq=}} located in {{ill|Atsuta Ward|ja|熱田区|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Atsuta Ward|qq=}}, {{ill|Nagoya|ja|名古屋市|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nagoya|qq=}}, {{ill|Aichi Prefecture|ja|愛知県|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Aichi Prefecture|qq=}}, Japan. It is one of the {{ill|sessha|ja|摂社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/sessha|qq=}} (subsidiary shrines) within {{ill|Atsuta Shrine|ja|熱田神宮|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Atsuta Shrine|qq=}}.

== deity ==
The enshrined deity is '''{{ill|Susanoo-no-Mikoto|ja|素盞嗚尊|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Susanoo-no-Mikoto|qq=}}''' (スサノオノミコト).<ref name="HP Minami Shingū-sha">[https://atsutajingu.or.jp/jingu/about/keidai/36.html Minami Shingū-sha | Atsuta Shrine Official Site] (Accessed June 28, 2018)</ref>

In pre-modern times, the shrine was known for enshrining '''{{ill|Gozu Tennō|ja|牛頭天王|1=Gozu Tennō|1=Gozu Tennō|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gozu Tennō|13=User:Immanuelle/Gozu Tennō|qq=}}''' (ごずてんのう), a deity syncretized with Susanoo and regarded as an {{ill|Epidemic deity|ja|疫神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Epidemic deity|qq=}}.{{#tag:ref|''"Minami Shingū is Gozu Tennō itself."'' (''Atsuta Daijingū Shintai Den''){{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-hen|2002|p=159}}|group="Note"}} The shrine is believed to have been established as a {{ill|Gion Shrine|ja|祇園神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gion Shrine|qq=}} (present-day {{ill|Yasaka Shrine|ja|八坂神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yasaka Shrine|qq=}}).{{#tag:ref|''"Gozu Tennō was enshrined here, and it was said to be a shrine associated with Gion."'' (''Atsumi-sō''){{Sfn|Atsumi-sō|1911|p=31}}|group="Note"}} It was also commonly referred to as ''Tennō-sha'' (天王社).{{#tag:ref|Mentioned in ''Atsumi-sō''{{Sfn|Atsumi-sō|1911|p=31}} and ''Atsuta Honsha Massha Shintai Sonmeiki''{{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=66}}|group="Note"}} Some theories suggest it was a branch shrine of {{ill|Tsushima Shrine|ja|津島神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsushima Shrine|qq=}},{{Sfn|Shinshū City History 2|1998|p=446}} a major shrine of the {{ill|Tsushima faith|ja|津島信仰|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsushima faith|qq=}}. The associated {{ill|Honji Suijaku|ja|本地垂迹|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Honji Suijaku|qq=}} deity was {{ill|Yakushi Nyorai|ja|薬師如来|1=Bhaisajyaguru|lt=Yakushi Nyorai (Me)dicine Buddha|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yakushi Nyorai|13=User:Immanuelle/Bhaisajyaguru|qq=}}.{{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-hen|2002|p=159}}

== History ==
The founding date is uncertain, with some records suggesting {{ill|1000|ja|1000th year|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1000|qq=}} (Chōhō 2) or {{ill|1023|ja|1023rd year|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1023|qq=}} (Jian 3).{{#tag:ref|''Atsuta Daijingū Shintai Den Monogatarigaki''{{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=179}}|group="Note"}} During the reign of {{ill|Emperor Ichijō|ja|一条天皇|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Emperor Ichijō|qq=}}, Japan experienced a severe {{ill|epidemic|ja|疫病|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/epidemic|qq=}}, and in Kyoto, a {{ill|Goryō-e|ja|御霊会|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Goryō-e|qq=}} was held in {{ill|1001|ja|1001st year|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1001|qq=}}.{{#tag:ref|''"On the 9th day of the 5th month, a ritual was held at {{ill|Shinano|ja|紫野|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinano|qq=}} to pacify the epidemic deity. It was called Goryō-e."''
(''Nihon Kiryaku,'' later Ichijō Tennō section){{Sfn|Kokushi Taikei 5|1897|pp=1046-1047}}|group="Note"}} It is believed that Minami Shingū-sha was established in response to this epidemic crisis.{{#tag:ref|''"In the third year of Jian (1023), an epidemic spread nationwide, so it was enshrined for pacification."''
(''Shaden''){{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=245}}|group="Note"}} However, little is known about the shrine's activities during the {{ill|Heian period|ja|平安時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Heian period|qq=}} and {{ill|Kamakura period|ja|鎌倉時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kamakura period|qq=}}.

The shrine was reconstructed in {{ill|1291|ja|1291st year|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1291|qq=}} (Shōō 4),{{Sfn|City History: Shrines and Temples|1915|p=161}} and a document from {{ill|1431|ja|1431st year|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1431|qq=}} (Eikyō 3) mentions "Minami Shingū."{{Sfn|Fukada|1898|p=18}} Some scholars theorize that Minami Shingū-sha did not exist until the mid-{{ill|Muromachi period|ja|室町時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Muromachi period|qq=}}, and that it was originally the site of {{ill|Magowaka Mikoshi Shrine|ja|孫若御子神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Magowaka Mikoshi Shrine|qq=}}, which was later replaced by Minami Shingū-sha under the patronage of the {{ill|Oda clan|ja|織田氏|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Oda clan|qq=}}, who were devout worshippers of Gozu Tennō.{{Sfn|Tsuda|1916|p=71}}

Despite being a subsidiary shrine of {{ill|Atsuta Shrine|ja|熱田神宮|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Atsuta Shrine|qq=}}, Minami Shingū-sha had a unique role as a deity of epidemics and was a major center for popular {{ill|Gion faith|ja|祇園信仰|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gion faith|qq=}} and {{ill|Tsushima faith|ja|津島信仰|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsushima faith|qq=}}. It was also the local {{ill|Ujigami|ja|氏神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ujigami|qq=}} for the merchants and commoners of Atsuta.{{Sfn|Historic Sites and Cultural Properties|1991|p=70}}

In {{ill|1686|ja|1686th year|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1686|qq=}} (Jōkyō 3), during a major restoration of Atsuta Shrine under the supervision of {{ill|Sakai Tadaakira|ja|酒井忠挙|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sakai Tadaakira|qq=}}, doubts were raised about the legitimacy of Minami Shingū-sha. However, the shrine’s existence was defended by Atsuta Shrine officials who described it as an important "divine mystery" (神秘).{{#tag:ref|''"Minami Shingū is a shrine of special divine mystery."''
(''Atsuta Miya Gozoei Jōkyōki''){{Sfn|Zōei Senkū-hen Jō|1980|p=195}}|group="Note"}}

