'''Minami Shingū-sha''' (南新宮社) is a [[:en:Shinto shrine|Shinto shrine]] located in [[:en:Atsuta-ku, Nagoya|Atsuta Ward]], [[:en:Nagoya|Nagoya]], [[:en:Aichi Prefecture|Aichi Prefecture]], Japan. It is one of the {{ill|sessha|ja|摂社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/sessha|qq=}} (subsidiary shrines) within [[:en:Atsuta Shrine|Atsuta Shrine]].

== deity ==
The enshrined deity is '''{{ill|Susanoo-no-Mikoto|ja|素盞嗚尊|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Susanoo-no-Mikoto|qq=}}''' (スサノオノミコト).<ref name="HP Minami Shingū-sha">[https://atsutajingu.or.jp/jingu/about/keidai/36.html Minami Shingū-sha | Atsuta Shrine Official Site] (Accessed June 28, 2018)</ref>

In pre-modern times, the shrine was known for enshrining '''[[:en:Gozu Tennō|Gozu Tennō]]''' (ごずてんのう), a deity syncretized with Susanoo and regarded as an {{ill|Epidemic deity|ja|疫神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Epidemic deity|qq=}}.{{#tag:ref|''"Minami Shingū is Gozu Tennō itself."'' (''Atsuta Daijingū Shintai Den''){{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-hen|2002|p=159}}|group="Note"}} The shrine is believed to have been established as a {{ill|Gion Shrine|ja|祇園神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gion Shrine|qq=}} (present-day [[:en:Yasaka Shrine|Yasaka Shrine]]).{{#tag:ref|''"Gozu Tennō was enshrined here, and it was said to be a shrine associated with Gion."'' (''Atsumi-sō''){{Sfn|Atsumi-sō|1911|p=31}}|group="Note"}} It was also commonly referred to as ''Tennō-sha'' (天王社).{{#tag:ref|Mentioned in ''Atsumi-sō''{{Sfn|Atsumi-sō|1911|p=31}} and ''Atsuta Honsha Massha Shintai Sonmeiki''{{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=66}}|group="Note"}} Some theories suggest it was a branch shrine of [[:en:Tsushima Shrine|Tsushima Shrine]],{{Sfn|Shinshū City History 2|1998|p=446}} a major shrine of the {{ill|Tsushima Faith|ja|津島信仰|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsushima faith|qq=}}. The associated [[:en:Honji suijaku|Honji Suijaku]] deity was [[:en:Bhaisajyaguru|Yakushi Nyorai Medicine Buddha]].{{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-hen|2002|p=159}}

== History ==
The founding date is uncertain, with some records suggesting [[:en:AD 1000|1000]] (Chōhō 2) or [[:en:1023|1023]] (Jian 3).{{#tag:ref|''Atsuta Daijingū Shintai Den Monogatarigaki''{{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=179}}|group="Note"}} During the reign of [[:en:Emperor Ichijō|Emperor Ichijō]], Japan experienced a severe {{ill|epidemic|ja|疫病|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/epidemic|qq=}}, and in Kyoto, a {{ill|Goryō-e|ja|御霊会|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Goryō-e|qq=}} was held in [[:en:1001|1001]].{{#tag:ref|''"On the 9th day of the 5th month, a ritual was held at {{ill|Shinano|ja|紫野|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinano|qq=}} to pacify the epidemic deity. It was called Goryō-e."''
(''Nihon Kiryaku,'' later Ichijō Tennō section){{Sfn|Kokushi Taikei 5|1897|pp=1046-1047}}|group="Note"}} It is believed that Minami Shingū-sha was established in response to this epidemic crisis.{{#tag:ref|''"In the third year of Jian (1023), an epidemic spread nationwide, so it was enshrined for pacification."''
(''Shaden''){{Sfn|Engi Yuiisho-zokuhen1|2005|p=245}}|group="Note"}} However, little is known about the shrine's activities during the [[:en:Heian period|Heian period]] and [[:en:Kamakura period|Kamakura period]].

The shrine was reconstructed in [[:en:1291|1291]] (Shōō 4),{{Sfn|City History: Shrines and Temples|1915|p=161}} and a document from [[:en:1431|1431]] (Eikyō 3) mentions "Minami Shingū."{{Sfn|Fukada|1898|p=18}} Some scholars theorize that Minami Shingū-sha did not exist until the mid-[[:en:Muromachi period|Muromachi period]], and that it was originally the site of {{ill|Magowaka Mikoshi Shrine|ja|孫若御子神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Magowaka Mikoshi Shrine|qq=}}, which was later replaced by Minami Shingū-sha under the patronage of the [[:en:Oda clan|Oda clan]], who were devout worshippers of Gozu Tennō.{{Sfn|Tsuda|1916|p=71}}

Despite being a subsidiary shrine of [[:en:Atsuta Shrine|Atsuta Shrine]], Minami Shingū-sha had a unique role as a deity of epidemics and was a major center for popular [[:en:Gion cult|Gion faith]] and {{ill|Tsushima Faith|ja|津島信仰|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsushima faith|qq=}}. It was also the local [[:en:Ujigami|Ujigami]] for the merchants and commoners of Atsuta.{{Sfn|Historic Sites and Cultural Properties|1991|p=70}}

In [[:en:1686|1686]] (Jōkyō 3), during a major restoration of Atsuta Shrine under the supervision of {{ill|Sakai Tadaakira|ja|酒井忠挙|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sakai Tadaakira|qq=}}, doubts were raised about the legitimacy of Minami Shingū-sha. However, the shrine’s existence was defended by Atsuta Shrine officials who described it as an important "divine mystery" (神秘).{{#tag:ref|''"Minami Shingū is a shrine of special divine mystery."''
(''Atsuta Miya Gozoei Jōkyōki''){{Sfn|Zōei Senkū-hen Jō|1980|p=195}}|group="Note"}}

